#{[{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046665} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406819} :activity/id #uuid "70dc8b50-8f87-5b55-9f2e-265dbd1adbfc" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050033 :topic/id #uuid "26b9f4f0-7964-52b3-9a23-d31ae6927cb5" :topic/math-academy-id 1939} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406819 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406817 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1dd5310c-dfc2-5a7d-bac5-5825f5064da6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406806} {:answer/id #uuid "4bd3692c-18d7-5dde-9e79-ca2c71d664a2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406807} {:answer/id #uuid "cd07008c-2d5e-5372-aa7f-53d2714271f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-5\\,i+2\\,j-2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406809} {:answer/id #uuid "e415ebda-6949-5999-a629-48685dbda1c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "-2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406812} {:answer/id #uuid "e8a7f1a1-3119-55e9-ab80-51ececd277a7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2\\,i-5\\,j+2\\,k" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406807} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f78fc66-8357-5b97-ab1a-5d79ee0e7c85" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406815}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "077d2dca-c9d8-5043-92cb-18878e5ddc66" :question/math-academy-id "q-71397" :question/problem "For the function $f(x,y,z)=2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy$, find a vector that's perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$" :question/worked-solution "A normal vector to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1)$ can be found by computing \n$∇f(-2,-3,1).$ \n\nSo, we first find the gradient and evaluate it at $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y,z) & ={f}_{x}(x,y,z)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y,z)\\text{ }j+{f}_{z}(x,y,z)\\text{ }k \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,j+\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2{x}^{2}+{z}^{2}-xy)\\,k \\\\  & =(4x-y)\\text{ }i-x\\text{ }j+2z\\text{ }k \\\\ ∇f(-2,-3,1) & =(4(-2)-(-3))\\,i-(-2)\\,j+2(1)\\,k \\\\  & =-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $-5\\,i+2\\,j+2\\,k$ is perpendicular to the level surface that passes through the point where $(x,y,z)=(-2,-3,1).$"} :step/id #uuid "6c546df4-95bc-53a6-8314-f63e977926a5" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406820}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406820 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406818 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a1d5e01-e81c-5544-a929-055747dc7f77" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x+y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406805} {:answer/id #uuid "82778c6a-7758-50ec-ad08-3720a9d490ae" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x-y-3=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406808} {:answer/id #uuid "cdae5c41-27fa-5c55-9f46-cceeabd59a1a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x+y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406810} {:answer/id #uuid "dbcc8cce-7237-5eb6-ac41-6a5901ea8c81" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x+2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406811} {:answer/id #uuid "ecf6508f-66f3-52c3-8fbf-477ac88383db" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x-y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406814}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406810} :answer-field/id #uuid "f9e7ba4e-7d61-5628-8ce5-894dfba410f8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406816}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "ef115c0b-3981-5c8f-8e9d-f41c20799c2d" :question/math-academy-id "q-130719" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $2y-x=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}).$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2y-x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y}) \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x,$ our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$  \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(\\frac{x}{2y})-2y+x)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot \\frac{1}{2y}+1)i+(\\frac{2y}{x}\\cdot (-\\frac{x}{2{y}^{2}})-2)j \\\\  & =(\\frac{1}{x}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{y}-2)j \\\\ ∇f(2,1) & =(\\frac{1}{2}+1)i+(-\\frac{1}{1}-2)j \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}\\,i-3\\,j \\\\  & =⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, $∇f(3,1)=⟨\\frac{3}{2},-3⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{(\\frac{3}{2})} & =\\frac{y-1}{(-3)} \\\\ -3(x-2) & =\\frac{3}{2}(y-1) \\\\ -6(x-2) & =3(y-1) \\\\ -2(x-2) & =y-1 \\\\ -2x+4 & =y-1 \\\\ 2x+y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "854e10c9-a2d4-5612-8167-ef459f663827" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406821}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186406821 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186265967 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0029eb5c-a868-5b35-b213-3fa6e3a04a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "2x-y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263011} {:answer/id #uuid "2d9853cb-1b4d-5d99-b740-f207d47a13c3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x-3y-5=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263390} {:answer/id #uuid "62cb3b0d-c5e0-5140-9b14-0b73eecbc9ac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x+y-1=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263860} {:answer/id #uuid "70e32100-046e-5e0e-b9f2-eac7fe211b6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x-2y-4=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186263982} {:answer/id #uuid "b5d5900d-9aee-5b56-aa97-4e95d9c8f10a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "x+2y=0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186264581}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186263011} :answer-field/id #uuid "263638a6-9832-58e0-a5f7-da0d13d29e2d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186265322}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "d42d4bf9-4eef-57b6-b4ed-d5448bfe2b61" :question/math-academy-id "q-130723" :question/problem "Consider the plane curve $4xy+2{x}^{2}=x+2y.$  Find the equation of the normal line to the curve at the point $(2,-1).$" :question/worked-solution "First, we bring all variables to one side: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4xy+2{x}^{2} & =x+2y \\\\ 4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIf we set $f(x,y)=4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y$, our curve can be viewed as the level curve $f(x,y)=0.$   \n\nA normal vector to the level curve that passes through the point $(2,-1)$ can be found by computing $∇f(2,-1).$ \n\nSo, we find the gradient and evaluate it at $(2,-1):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}∇f(x,y) & ={f}_{x}(x,y)\\,i+{f}_{y}(x,y)\\,j \\\\  & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,i+\\frac{∂}{∂y}(4xy+2{x}^{2}-x-2y)\\,j \\\\  & =(4y+4x-1)\\,i+(4x-2)\\,j \\\\ ∇f(2,-1) & =(4(-1)+4(2)-1)\\,i+(4(2)-2)\\,j \\\\  & =3\\,i+6\\,j \\\\  & =⟨3,6⟩\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n Hence, $∇f(2,-1)=⟨3,6⟩$ is a normal vector to the curve at $(2,-1).$\n\nFinally, the Cartesian equation of the line that is parallel to $v=⟨{v}_{x},{v}_{y}⟩$ and passes through the point $({x}_{0},{y}_{0})$ can be written as: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{x-{x}_{0}}{{v}_{x}} & =\\frac{y-{y}_{0}}{{v}_{y}} \\\\ \\frac{x-2}{3} & =\\frac{y-(-1)}{6} \\\\ 6(x-2) & =3(y+1) \\\\ 2(x-2) & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-4 & =y+1 \\\\ 2x-y-5 & =0\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9536b8a9-959c-5b6e-8ff0-53d077d7ad52"}] :activity/title "The Gradient as a Normal Vector" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186406804}]}
